d #uuid "90c1007a-4679-560a-84f7-593c87ce0e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282941} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac19e51-1f63-5f51-98b2-8211bafdd295" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48320d50-11dc-5c25-b211-1a6293887c53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282299} :answer-field/id #uuid "69141054-77da-5506-94da-51c42eb49c54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72644873-b2ba-57a8-bd4c-d5dc5e8e5941" :question/math-academy-id "q-167609" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x+y$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨t,\\,1+t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=⟨t,\\,1+t⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=t$ and $y=1+t.$    \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)j \\\\  & =\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =t+(1+t)=2t+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+1)\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2t+1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{[{t}^{2}+t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(1+1-0) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284593 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "481a6b6e-05a2-5003-9ac7-b4876ffa4ffa" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Scalar Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10206875-a5c4-5022-9ee9-43b3a21b42b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281836} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd849f2-6a5c-5409-a292-f59a3dc7a36a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282215} {:answer/id #uuid "4544a980-713e-5e34-893c-6b08076a1997" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282275} {:answer/id #uuid "7f63b4af-1a8d-5157-bbb0-dc8287c37b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282786} {:answer/id #uuid "e1a5f08d-e51f-5026-8bb2-63d5b7342f7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f63b4af-1a8d-5157-bbb0-dc8287c37b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282786} :answer-field/id #uuid "b92feada-828f-5f43-8105-e8387a4f1f6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2907fc18-ef26-5825-bc1f-b4cb4bdbf7cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-167621" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}(2{x}^{2}-16)\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$  \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-16$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-16 \\\\  & =16(2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1) \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(2{x}^{2}-16)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =64{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =64{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =32[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0] \\\\  & =32(1-0) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "481a6b6e-05a2-5003-9ac7-b4876ffa4ffa" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Scalar Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2157cbec-dd3c-50c6-8f66-e922ba1f66ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342514} {:answer/id #uuid "46847ff5-553b-5f26-9805-09a27496f196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342819} {:answer/id #uuid "52b6665a-0ccb-5b28-87e4-9cb8ee2700ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi -1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342928} {:answer/id #uuid "a88c1662-aadb-5062-a115-55611ab6c159" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343722} {:answer/id #uuid "bfef22ae-f97c-5172-89a1-1bc5619b881d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46847ff5-553b-5f26-9805-09a27496f196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342819} :answer-field/id #uuid "09fe84d2-3042-5159-a1de-85adb6692d1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5649582e-ddb6-5db4-a7c3-699a007eaafe" :question/math-academy-id "q-76433" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$  \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={y}^{2}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}=4{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{[t-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{2}]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4[(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{1}{2})-0] \\\\  & =\\pi -2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "481a6b6e-05a2-5003-9ac7-b4876ffa4ffa" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Scalar Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d7aeab2-63a6-55b6-9670-af11f7878d7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342743} {:answer/id #uuid "55aaa22c-0a54-5244-a488-f06f0ea67eda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342950} {:answer/id #uuid "7df5078f-cc9c-5640-bdbe-c73ad5edaf2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343323} {:answer/id #uuid "d44736e7-181c-5708-8fc6-091f82bf604a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344100} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc86749-0a58-547d-b292-dd0c15c33cf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55aaa22c-0a54-5244-a488-f06f0ea67eda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342950} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddbabc5e-406f-59f6-a3fe-8b87b2e1d083" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a7a51cb6-cac0-57a7-bccc-fcb3f00d4af4" :question/math-academy-id "q-76434" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y-x}$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨t,\\,t-4⟩$ for $t\\in [0,4].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=⟨t,\\,t-4⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=t$ and $y=t-4.$     \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-4)j \\\\  & =i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y-x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{t-(4-t)}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\,\\cdot \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}t\\,{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\cdot (4-0) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "481a6b6e-05a2-5003-9ac7-b4876ffa4ffa" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Scalar Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "330a0610-7510-562c-a947-1ed175f73e6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342669} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec7f114-71dd-5707-a837-49ec4e0f58d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343037} {:answer/id #uuid "784dc8af-a9a4-53e0-8947-d481e7980aa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343277} {:answer/id #uuid "7f5af1a3-65b7-5d58-b8b9-b68cc63abf8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343333} {:answer/id #uuid "e3af6d11-71f3-548a-854f-ff88ce98cd06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "330a0610-7510-562c-a947-1ed175f73e6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342669} :answer-field/id #uuid "b827e50e-d12a-5657-bed2-ba45d90ca8cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6c30ef5-4b00-5501-a5b6-18892cf4af06" :question/math-academy-id "q-76447" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{3}-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $r(t)=4{t}^{2}\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j$ for $0\\le t\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=4{t}^{2}\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x=4{t}^{2}$ and $y=3{t}^{2}.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})j \\\\  & =8t\\,i+6t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(8t)^{2}+(6t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64{t}^{2}+36{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =10|t| \\\\  & =10t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t|=t$ since $t>0$ along the path of $C.$ \n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{3}-2{y}^{3}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={(4{t}^{2})}^{3}-2{(3{t}^{2})}^{3} \\\\  & ={4}^{3}{t}^{6}-2\\cdot {3}^{3}{t}^{6} \\\\  & =64{t}^{6}-54{t}^{6} \\\\  & =10{t}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{3}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}10{t}^{6}\\cdot 10t\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =100{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{7}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{{t}^{8}}{8}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =100[\\frac{{1}^{8}}{8}+\\frac{{0}^{8}}{8}] \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & =12.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520663 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "481a6b6e-05a2-5003-9ac7-b4876ffa4ffa" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Scalar Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43ad5b43-aace-5d3d-a358-724cb263373e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2688}{5} - \\frac{784384 \\sqrt{2}}{945}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520675} {:answer/id #uuid "488a74d3-7dcc-59c5-a18f-dfc26f0e4a2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8 - \\frac{128 \\sqrt{2}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520676} {:answer/id #uuid "ba09aabf-f5f1-5b41-8fdb-b6dacd6f5500" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520707} {:answer/id #uuid "dac02ed1-55d3-5134-9bb8-1308a80f0326" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{8704 \\sqrt{2}}{105}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520720} {:answer/id #uuid "e19d4737-fdde-54f7-b02d-3480dff1be65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64 - \\frac{8704 \\sqrt{2}}{105}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e19d4737-fdde-54f7-b02d-3480dff1be65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64 - \\frac{8704 \\sqrt{2}}{105}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520723} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1997853-2be2-51a6-9186-222b0926a788" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "145cd2cc-e119-5363-8df0-50511cd77f67" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=- x - 3 y$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨\\frac{16 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3},\\,2 t^{2} - 4 t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=⟨\\frac{16 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3},\\,2 t^{2} - 4 t⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=\\frac{16 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}$ and $y=2 t^{2} - 4 t.$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{16 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t^{2} - 4 t)j \\\\ &=(8 \\sqrt{t})i+(4 t - 4)j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(8 \\sqrt{t})^2+(4 t - 4)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{64t+16(t-1)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{16(t^2+2t+1)} \\\\ &=4|t+1| \\\\ &=4 t + 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t+1|=t+1$ since $t\\geq0$ along the path of $C.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=- x - 3 y$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=(-1)(\\frac{16 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3})+(-3)(2 t^{2} - 4 t) \\\\ &=- \\frac{16 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3} - 6 t^{2} + 12 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C - x - 3 y\\,\\text{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2}(- \\frac{16 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3} - 6 t^{2} + 12 t)\\cdot (4 t + 4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2}- \\frac{64}{3}t^{3/2}(t+1)+(-48)(t^2/2-t)(t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2}- \\frac{64 t^{\\frac{5}{2}}}{3} - \\frac{64 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3} - 24 t^{3} + 24 t^{2} + 48 t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[(- \\frac{128 t^{\\frac{7}{2}}}{21})+(- \\frac{128 t^{\\frac{5}{2}}}{15})+(- 6 t^{4})+(8 t^{3})+(24 t^{2})\\right]_{0}^{2} \\\\ &=\\left((- \\frac{128 (2)^{\\frac{7}{2}}}{21})+(- \\frac{128 (2)^{\\frac{5}{2}}}{15})+(- 6 (2)^{4})+(8 (2)^{3})+(24 (2)^{2})\\right)-\\left((- \\frac{128 (0)^{\\frac{7}{2}}}{21})+(- \\frac{128 (0)^{\\frac{5}{2}}}{15})+(- 6 (0)^{4})+(8 (0)^{3})+(24 (0)^{2})\\right) \\\\ &=\\left((- \\frac{1024 \\sqrt{2}}{21})+(- \\frac{512 \\sqrt{2}}{15})+(-96)+(64)+(96)\\right)-\\left((0)+(0)+(0)+(0)+(0)\\right) \\\\ &=64 - \\frac{8704 \\sqrt{2}}{105}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520666 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "481a6b6e-05a2-5003-9ac7-b4876ffa4ffa" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Scalar Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "400cf204-a287-54da-8cf6-8f6c5520cca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520673} {:answer/id #uuid "a20dac01-182b-507b-bf2f-639fa423045a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "940" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520700} {:answer/id #uuid "aa3bf514-31bd-5be2-b51b-27b23cca2950" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{212050}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520703} {:answer/id #uuid "adb18c45-c4c6-5d1a-bc95-d4057d6c1d2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1582}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520704} {:answer/id #uuid "d53c1a86-d5cc-5a33-a7f3-31c1f8e70e6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5940" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d53c1a86-d5cc-5a33-a7f3-31c1f8e70e6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5940" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520718} :answer-field/id #uuid "67baca2f-3def-5b74-8464-fcb9eed2a55b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2136d6d7-0ed7-55ff-b190-1b731f49a8bb" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} + 2 y^{2} - 3$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨5 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 5 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,- 5 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 5 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩$ for $t\\in [1,3].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=⟨5 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 5 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,- 5 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 5 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=5 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 5 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=- 5 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 5 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 5 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 5 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 5 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\ &=(5)(-\\sin t+\\sin t+t\\cos t)i+(5)(\\cos t-\\cos t+t\\sin t)j \\\\ &=(5 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})i+(5 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(5 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2+(5 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{25t^2(\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t)} \\\\ &=5|t| \\\\ &=5 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t|=t$ since $t\\geq0$ along the path of $C.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} + 2 y^{2} - 3$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=(2)\\left((5 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 5 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2+(- 5 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 5 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2\\right)+(-3) \\\\ &=(50)\\left(\\cos^2t+2t\\sin t\\cos t+t^2\\sin^2t+\\sin^2t-2t\\sin t\\cos t+t^2\\cos^2t\\right)+(-3) \\\\ &=(50)(1+t^2)+(-3) \\\\ &=50 t^{2} + 47\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 2 x^{2} + 2 y^{2} - 3\\,\\text{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{3}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{3}(50 t^{2} + 47)\\cdot (5 t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{3}250 t^{3} + 235 t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[(\\frac{235 t^{2}}{2})+(\\frac{125 t^{4}}{2})\\right]_{1}^{3} \\\\ &=\\left((\\frac{235 (3)^{2}}{2})+(\\frac{125 (3)^{4}}{2})\\right)-\\left((\\frac{235 (1)^{2}}{2})+(\\frac{125 (1)^{4}}{2})\\right) \\\\ &=\\left((\\frac{2115}{2})+(\\frac{10125}{2})\\right)-\\left((\\frac{235}{2})+(\\frac{125}{2})\\right) \\\\ &=5940.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520670 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "481a6b6e-05a2-5003-9ac7-b4876ffa4ffa" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Scalar Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c4d59fc-515b-5f6a-9dc6-509c6e675345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520665} {:answer/id #uuid "48c82739-041c-56fa-8753-1bfa7fa0ee17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520677} {:answer/id #uuid "5e065b1b-4ec3-533f-9c2b-6e064e094ed4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520681} {:answer/id #uuid "797a833b-97df-53a4-a250-ba3c8eab7778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520692} {:answer/id #uuid "d047b09d-1678-5550-904c-27b96dd5e577" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e065b1b-4ec3-533f-9c2b-6e064e094ed4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520681} :answer-field/id #uuid "dffa612e-a99a-5518-aa59-ddebaf8c26ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2cfdc280-62d5-528b-8c5c-9974a8b2cb2c" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=2 x + 2 y$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨- 2 t,\\,- 2 t - 2⟩$ for $t\\in [-2,0].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=⟨- 2 t,\\,- 2 t - 2⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=- 2 t$ and $y=- 2 t - 2.$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 2 t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 2 t - 2)j \\\\ &=(-2)i+(-2)j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(-2)^2+(-2)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{8} \\\\ &=2 \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=2 x + 2 y$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=(2)\\left(- 2 t+(- 2 t - 2)\\right) \\\\ &=- 8 t - 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 2 x + 2 y\\,\\text{d}s&=\\int_{-2}^{0}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{-2}^{0}(- 8 t - 4)\\cdot (2 \\sqrt{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=(2 \\sqrt{2})\\int_{-2}^{0}- 8 t - 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{-2}^{0}- 16 \\sqrt{2} t - 8 \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=(4 \\sqrt{2})\\left[- 2 t^{2}+(- 2 t)\\right]_{-2}^{0} \\\\ &=\\left[(- 8 \\sqrt{2} t^{2})+(- 8 \\sqrt{2} t)\\right]_{-2}^{0} \\\\ &=\\left((- 8 \\sqrt{2} (0)^{2})+(- 8 \\sqrt{2} (0))\\right)-\\left((- 8 \\sqrt{2} (-2)^{2})+(- 8 \\sqrt{2} (-2))\\right) \\\\ &=\\left((0)+(0)\\right)-\\left((- 32 \\sqrt{2})+(16 \\sqrt{2})\\right) \\\\ &=16 \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520686 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "481a6b6e-05a2-5003-9ac7-b4876ffa4ffa" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Scalar Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03873494-83c0-5778-9053-50e3cd0a8960" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520659} {:answer/id #uuid "067f6b16-2ef2-5fc3-a21a-839c972f0942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2} - \\frac{27 \\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520661} {:answer/id #uuid "4f2be384-eb81-581d-887c-e86a86517461" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520680} {:answer/id #uuid "74ba6b0b-7f7b-585f-8489-1ebb8057a589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520689} {:answer/id #uuid "c6be7094-4eda-5791-84ae-661427999cac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6be7094-4eda-5791-84ae-661427999cac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520713} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b93d243-d980-5d5b-a615-915dffb75464" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b0f1fc6-a816-51a9-b852-0409900ea7be" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=9 - 2 x^{2}$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩$ for $t\\in [- \\frac{\\pi}{2},- \\frac{\\pi}{4}].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=⟨3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\ &=(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})i+(- 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2+(- 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{9\\sin^2t+9\\cos^2t} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{9(\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t)} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{9} \\\\ &=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=9 - 2 x^{2}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=(-1)\\left(2(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2-9\\right) \\\\ &=(-9)(2\\cos^2t-1) \\\\ &=- 9 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 9 - 2 x^{2}\\,\\text{d}s&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}(- 9 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)})\\cdot (3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}- 27 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=-27\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}\\cos 2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=(-27)\\left[\\frac{1}{2}\\sin 2t\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}} \\\\ &=\\left[(- \\frac{27 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2})\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}} \\\\ &=\\left((- \\frac{27 \\sin{\\left(2 (- \\frac{\\pi}{4}) \\right)}}{2})\\right)-\\left((- \\frac{27 \\sin{\\left(2 (- \\frac{\\pi}{2}) \\right)}}{2})\\right) \\\\ &=\\left((\\frac{27}{2})\\right)-\\left((0)\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{27}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "481a6b6e-05a2-5003-9ac7-b4876ffa4ffa" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Scalar Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26cafdf9-bb1b-5db6-9f2c-63c617017df2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520667} {:answer/id #uuid "3d86a63d-975f-514b-aa2d-6a9a2dda1a1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520672} {:answer/id #uuid "649c7469-e587-53cb-ad3a-e1aa2a960ad5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520682} {:answer/id #uuid "8b6f206b-ea3f-54ac-8df3-223331b388be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520696} {:answer/id #uuid "f6004e5c-0f89-546c-9a29-395a9815c45a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d86a63d-975f-514b-aa2d-6a9a2dda1a1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520672} :answer-field/id #uuid "94de007f-b740-55aa-979f-480b58ced30a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d171fb6-8597-5d32-b391-80e23689046f" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=- 2 x - 2 y$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨- t,\\,1 - t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,3].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=⟨- t,\\,1 - t⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=- t$ and $y=1 - t.$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1 - t)j \\\\ &=(-1)i+(-1)j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(-1)^2+(-1)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=- 2 x - 2 y$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=(-2)\\left(- t+(1 - t)\\right) \\\\ &=4 t - 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C - 2 x - 2 y\\,\\text{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{3}(4 t - 2)\\cdot (\\sqrt{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=(\\sqrt{2})\\int_{0}^{3}4 t - 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{3}4 \\sqrt{2} t - 2 \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=(- 2 \\sqrt{2})\\left[- t^{2}+(t)\\right]_{0}^{3} \\\\ &=\\left[(2 \\sqrt{2} t^{2})+(- 2 \\sqrt{2} t)\\right]_{0}^{3} \\\\ &=\\left((2 \\sqrt{2} (3)^{2})+(- 2 \\sqrt{2} (3))\\right)-\\left((2 \\sqrt{2} (0)^{2})+(- 2 \\sqrt{2} (0))\\right) \\\\ &=\\left((18 \\sqrt{2})+(- 6 \\sqrt{2})\\right)-\\left((0)+(0)\\right) \\\\ &=12 \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "481a6b6e-05a2-5003-9ac7-b4876ffa4ffa" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Scalar Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48c9bce6-a245-551e-932e-0da2c01469dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{32 \\pi^{3}}{3} - 34 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520678} {:answer/id #uuid "67d2b09b-2ddd-5417-8f12-3e433a7e207d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 68 \\pi^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520684} {:answer/id #uuid "8600936c-7807-533f-b7ec-6363eb423607" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32 \\pi^{2} \\left(- \\pi^{2} - 2\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520695} {:answer/id #uuid "afcc62a2-a04f-5e40-840e-4035cd3ad69e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi^{2} \\left(- 32 \\pi^{2} - 68\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520705} {:answer/id #uuid "ca7f42b2-a1b5-58b8-b1c4-f9b190a7088f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32 \\pi^{3} \\left(- 48 \\pi^{2} - 85\\right)}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afcc62a2-a04f-5e40-840e-4035cd3ad69e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi^{2} \\left(- 32 \\pi^{2} - 68\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520705} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3dd3542-87fa-5542-a06b-ffafba3d77a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7c78a6b5-a741-5677-9ee8-db5e516f2ee1" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2} - 2$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨4 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,- 4 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=⟨4 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,- 4 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=4 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=- 4 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 4 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\ &=(4)(-\\sin t+\\sin t+t\\cos t)i+(4)(\\cos t-\\cos t+t\\sin t)j \\\\ &=(4 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})i+(4 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(4 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2+(4 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{16t^2(\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t)} \\\\ &=4|t| \\\\ &=4 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t|=t$ since $t\\geq0$ along the path of $C.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2} - 2$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=(-2)\\left((4 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2+(- 4 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2\\right)+(-2) \\\\ &=(-32)\\left(\\cos^2t+2t\\sin t\\cos t+t^2\\sin^2t+\\sin^2t-2t\\sin t\\cos t+t^2\\cos^2t\\right)+(-2) \\\\ &=(-32)(1+t^2)+(-2) \\\\ &=- 32 t^{2} - 34\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C - 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2} - 2\\,\\text{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}(- 32 t^{2} - 34)\\cdot (4 t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}- 128 t^{3} - 136 t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[(- 68 t^{2})+(- 32 t^{4})\\right]_{0}^{\\pi} \\\\ &=\\left((- 68 (\\pi)^{2})+(- 32 (\\pi)^{4})\\right)-\\left((- 68 (0)^{2})+(- 32 (0)^{4})\\right) \\\\ &=\\left((- 68 \\pi^{2})+(- 32 \\pi^{4})\\right)-\\left((0)+(0)\\right) \\\\ &=\\pi^{2} \\left(- 32 \\pi^{2} - 68\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "481a6b6e-05a2-5003-9ac7-b4876ffa4ffa" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Scalar Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0651e1c4-08b2-560b-bab1-0ee3946b48cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{5245}{84} + \\frac{2720 \\sqrt{2}}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520660} {:answer/id #uuid "2d9a2970-315f-5d30-bdd2-2c7a3e0457b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6 + \\frac{32 \\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520671} {:answer/id #uuid "8ee0d219-cf7d-50b1-a2df-423d51c39b04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{160}{7} + \\frac{2720 \\sqrt{2}}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520697} {:answer/id #uuid "a5969666-b75b-5e6c-a017-710506b5e7b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{475}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520702} {:answer/id #uuid "c083f817-fabc-53b4-9149-8f9984d36ed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{127375}{189} + \\frac{306400 \\sqrt{2}}{189}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0651e1c4-08b2-560b-bab1-0ee3946b48cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{5245}{84} + \\frac{2720 \\sqrt{2}}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520660} :answer-field/id #uuid "763c684e-1374-5918-a1c0-00fb29101703" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dd9ec35c-d87b-5b5e-8742-da91c43c261f" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x + 2 y$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨\\frac{20 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3},\\,\\frac{5 t^{2}}{2} - 5 t⟩$ for $t\\in [1,2].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=⟨\\frac{20 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3},\\,\\frac{5 t^{2}}{2} - 5 t⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=\\frac{20 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}$ and $y=\\frac{5 t^{2}}{2} - 5 t.$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{20 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{5 t^{2}}{2} - 5 t)j \\\\ &=(10 \\sqrt{t})i+(5 t - 5)j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(10 \\sqrt{t})^2+(5 t - 5)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{100t+25(t-1)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{25(t^2+2t+1)} \\\\ &=5|t+1| \\\\ &=5 t + 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t+1|=t+1$ since $t\\geq0$ along the path of $C.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x + 2 y$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=(1)(\\frac{20 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3})+(2)(\\frac{5 t^{2}}{2} - 5 t) \\\\ &=\\frac{20 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3} + 5 t^{2} - 10 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x + 2 y\\,\\text{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{2}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{2}(\\frac{20 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3} + 5 t^{2} - 10 t)\\cdot (5 t + 5)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{2}\\frac{100}{3}t^{3/2}(t+1)+(50)(t^2/2-t)(t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{2}\\frac{100 t^{\\frac{5}{2}}}{3} + \\frac{100 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3} + 25 t^{3} - 25 t^{2} - 50 t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[(\\frac{200 t^{\\frac{7}{2}}}{21})+(\\frac{40 t^{\\frac{5}{2}}}{3})+(\\frac{25 t^{4}}{4})+(- \\frac{25 t^{3}}{3})+(- 25 t^{2})\\right]_{1}^{2} \\\\ &=\\left((\\frac{200 (2)^{\\frac{7}{2}}}{21})+(\\frac{40 (2)^{\\frac{5}{2}}}{3})+(\\frac{25 (2)^{4}}{4})+(- \\frac{25 (2)^{3}}{3})+(- 25 (2)^{2})\\right)-\\left((\\frac{200 (1)^{\\frac{7}{2}}}{21})+(\\frac{40 (1)^{\\frac{5}{2}}}{3})+(\\frac{25 (1)^{4}}{4})+(- \\frac{25 (1)^{3}}{3})+(- 25 (1)^{2})\\right) \\\\ &=\\left((\\frac{1600 \\sqrt{2}}{21})+(\\frac{160 \\sqrt{2}}{3})+(100)+(- \\frac{200}{3})+(-100)\\right)-\\left((\\frac{200}{21})+(\\frac{40}{3})+(\\frac{25}{4})+(- \\frac{25}{3})+(-25)\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{5245}{84} + \\frac{2720 \\sqrt{2}}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520724 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "481a6b6e-05a2-5003-9ac7-b4876ffa4ffa" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Scalar Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6809d42a-ff14-5b11-9e6c-a4c5dc0d1649" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32 - 8 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520685} {:answer/id #uuid "75f20079-bf9a-5643-beb4-8aac0887b679" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 - 8 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520690} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f5ade6-6985-5cb0-9970-b04d3a8c93cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 - 2 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520701} {:answer/id #uuid "ba75b0a0-7b52-5b4e-be6d-4643c72bed25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 8 \\pi - 16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520708} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd2e040-255e-5707-9f2d-893b48ce96dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64 - 32 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75f20079-bf9a-5643-beb4-8aac0887b679" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 - 8 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520690} :answer-field/id #uuid "da3668a3-a325-5a48-bc73-572e9e236b2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e231449f-d0a8-51fb-ac8b-87748d209fc9" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=- y^{2}$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩$ for $t\\in [- \\frac{\\pi}{4},0].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=⟨4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\ &=(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})i+(- 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2+(- 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{16\\sin^2t+16\\cos^2t} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{16(\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t)} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{16} \\\\ &=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=- y^{2}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=(-1)(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2 \\\\ &=-16\\sin^2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C - y^{2}\\,\\text{d}s&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{0}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{0}(- 16 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{0}- 64 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=-64\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{0}\\sin^2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=-64\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{0}\\frac{1-\\cos 2t}{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=-32\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{0}1-\\cos 2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=(-32)\\left[t-\\frac{\\sin 2t}{2}\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{0} \\\\ &=\\left[(- 32 t)+(16 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)})\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{0} \\\\ &=\\left((- 32 (0))+(16 \\sin{\\left(2 (0) \\right)})\\right)-\\left((- 32 (- \\frac{\\pi}{4}))+(16 \\sin{\\left(2 (- \\frac{\\pi}{4}) \\right)})\\right) \\\\ &=\\left((0)+(0)\\right)-\\left((8 \\pi)+(-16)\\right) \\\\ &=16 - 8 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "481a6b6e-05a2-5003-9ac7-b4876ffa4ffa" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Scalar Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "432eecfb-20a2-5095-b3ec-3e76f9097706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 216 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520674} {:answer/id #uuid "4cf41ec2-f489-5de9-a19a-a47e5625943d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 6 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520679} {:answer/id #uuid "6b48cc25-c525-5393-8d7c-54a84b821d56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520687} {:answer/id #uuid "82514b89-cc6b-5812-8d65-61e3a30d237a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520694} {:answer/id #uuid "9d4da34d-0d0a-5dea-b628-7489bccb6cc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cf41ec2-f489-5de9-a19a-a47e5625943d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 6 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520679} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf9a4aeb-294c-5c91-aea0-4c28b6189145" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e949b5e8-0b4c-52ac-b46c-4d2d9167f454" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=- \\frac{3}{- x + y}$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨3 t,\\,3 t + 6⟩$ for $t\\in [-2,2].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=⟨3 t,\\,3 t + 6⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=3 t$ and $y=3 t + 6.$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3 t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3 t + 6)j \\\\ &=(3)i+(3)j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(3)^2+(3)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{18} \\\\ &=3 \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=- \\frac{3}{- x + y}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=\\frac{-3}{(3 t + 6)-(3 t)} \\\\ &=\\frac{-3}{6} \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C - \\frac{3}{- x + y}\\,\\text{d}s&=\\int_{-2}^{2}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{-2}^{2}(- \\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (3 \\sqrt{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=(3 \\sqrt{2})\\int_{-2}^{2}- \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{-2}^{2}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2})\\left[t\\right]_{-2}^{2} \\\\ &=\\left[(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2} t}{2})\\right]_{-2}^{2} \\\\ &=\\left((- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2} (2)}{2})\\right)-\\left((- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2} (-2)}{2})\\right) \\\\ &=\\left((- 3 \\sqrt{2})\\right)-\\left((3 \\sqrt{2})\\right) \\\\ &=- 6 \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "481a6b6e-05a2-5003-9ac7-b4876ffa4ffa" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Scalar Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "145424bf-f6bb-5942-9d09-e285f663ac59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8 \\pi^{3} \\left(5 + 6 \\pi^{2}\\right)}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520662} {:answer/id #uuid "18d47090-2dd1-5291-bb5a-e6fbf0892724" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\pi + \\frac{4 \\pi^{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520664} {:answer/id #uuid "be74739c-3ea8-5708-9646-40ef9c2001d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\pi^{2} \\left(2 + \\pi^{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520709} {:answer/id #uuid "bed90742-ab65-5adc-a430-b09e0b81b132" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\pi^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520710} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb49685-bf7a-57be-992d-18ab3d8cc6d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\pi^{2} \\left(1 + \\pi^{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebb49685-bf7a-57be-992d-18ab3d8cc6d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\pi^{2} \\left(1 + \\pi^{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520726} :answer-field/id #uuid "289dda4e-7c4b-52ce-ba32-751594b39498" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ff8f8180-bdc0-5b6a-a9a7-ec6192daea60" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x^{2} + y^{2} - 2$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=⟨2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\ &=(2)(-\\sin t+\\sin t+t\\cos t)i+(2)(\\cos t-\\cos t+t\\sin t)j \\\\ &=(2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})i+(2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2+(2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{4t^2(\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t)} \\\\ &=2|t| \\\\ &=2 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t|=t$ since $t\\geq0$ along the path of $C.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x^{2} + y^{2} - 2$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=(1)\\left((2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2+(- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2\\right)+(-2) \\\\ &=(4)\\left(\\cos^2t+2t\\sin t\\cos t+t^2\\sin^2t+\\sin^2t-2t\\sin t\\cos t+t^2\\cos^2t\\right)+(-2) \\\\ &=(4)(1+t^2)+(-2) \\\\ &=4 t^{2} + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x^{2} + y^{2} - 2\\,\\text{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}(4 t^{2} + 2)\\cdot (2 t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}8 t^{3} + 4 t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[(2 t^{2})+(2 t^{4})\\right]_{0}^{\\pi} \\\\ &=\\left((2 (\\pi)^{2})+(2 (\\pi)^{4})\\right)-\\left((2 (0)^{2})+(2 (0)^{4})\\right) \\\\ &=\\left((2 \\pi^{2})+(2 \\pi^{4})\\right)-\\left((0)+(0)\\right) \\\\ &=2 \\pi^{2} \\left(1 + \\pi^{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Scalar Function"}]}
